# =============================================================================
# SIR — lodash.tolength
# =============================================================================
UNIT toLength
KIND FUNCTIONAL
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG toLength(value: any) -> number          # default export; module.exports = toLength
DEPENDS-ON none                              # zero deps; all helpers are inlined below
ORACLE value -> oracle.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RE-EMITTER DIRECTIVE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The original is a CommonJS default export: `module.exports = toLength`.
# The blind re-emitter MUST emit an ES module whose DEFAULT export is the function:
#     export default function toLength(value) { ... }
#   (or)  const toLength = ...; export default toLength;
# The function is PURE and READ-ONLY: it never mutates `value`, reads no ambient
# state (no clock/RNG/env/fs/net), and has no seam. Same input -> same output.
# Reconstruct EVERYTHING below inline; do NOT assume lodash is available.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONSTANTS (exact, from source)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INFINITY          = 1 / 0                        # i.e. +Infinity
# MAX_INTEGER       = 1.7976931348623157e+308      # == Number.MAX_VALUE
# NAN               = 0 / 0                         # i.e. NaN
# MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH  = 4294967295                    # == 2^32 - 1   (the clamp upper bound)
# symbolTag         = '[object Symbol]'
# reTrim     = /^\s+|\s+$/g                          # leading+trailing whitespace
# reIsBadHex = /^[-+]0x[0-9a-f]+$/i                  # SIGNED hex string -> rejected as NaN
# reIsBinary = /^0b[01]+$/i
# reIsOctal  = /^0o[0-7]+$/i
# freeParseInt = parseInt                            # the global parseInt
# objectToString = Object.prototype.toString

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BEHAVIOR — top-level pipeline (toLength)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toLength(value):
#   return value ? baseClamp(toInteger(value), 0, MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH) : 0
#
# NODE T1 (falsy short-circuit): if `value` is FALSY -> return 0 immediately.
#   JS falsy set: 0, -0, NaN, "", null, undefined, false, AND 0n (BigInt zero).
#   CRITICAL ORDERING CONSEQUENCE: the falsy test happens BEFORE any numeric
#   coercion, so falsy inputs NEVER reach toInteger/toNumber. This is why:
#       - NaN     -> 0      (falsy; short-circuits; never clamped)
#       - ""      -> 0      (falsy)
#       - 0 / -0  -> 0      (falsy; note: returns the literal 0, i.e. +0, NOT -0;
#                            Object.is(toLength(-0), 0) === true)
#       - 0n      -> 0      (BigInt zero is falsy -> short-circuit -> never throws)
#   The returned 0 here is the integer literal `0` (positive zero).
#
# NODE T2 (truthy): for any TRUTHY value -> return baseClamp(toInteger(value), 0, MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH).
#   Compute r = toInteger(value) first (see below), then clamp into [0, 4294967295].

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# baseClamp(number, lower, upper)   — here called as baseClamp(r, 0, 4294967295)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# baseClamp(number, lower, upper):
#   if (number === number) {                 # i.e. number is NOT NaN
#     if (upper !== undefined) number = number <= upper ? number : upper;   # cap at upper
#     if (lower !== undefined) number = number >= lower ? number : lower;   # floor at lower
#   }
#   return number
#
# With lower=0, upper=4294967295 (both defined):
#   - if r is NaN -> returned unchanged (NaN). BUT NaN cannot occur here in practice:
#     toInteger never returns NaN (it maps non-finite/NaN to 0), and the only way to
#     reach baseClamp is via the truthy branch. Still, define it faithfully: NaN passes through.
#   - else:  r > 4294967295        -> 4294967295   (cap)
#            r < 0                  -> 0            (floor; this is the integer literal 0 = +0)
#            0 <= r <= 4294967295   -> r            (unchanged)
#   ORDER: cap-to-upper is applied FIRST, then floor-to-lower. (Result is identical to
#   min/max here, but preserve the documented order.)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toInteger(value)  — truncate toward zero
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toInteger(value):
#   result    = toFinite(value)
#   remainder = result % 1
#   return result === result ? (remainder ? result - remainder : result) : 0
#
# Semantics:
#   - result = toFinite(value)  (always a FINITE number or 0; see toFinite).
#   - TRUNCATION TOWARD ZERO via (result - (result % 1)):
#       JS `%` keeps the sign of the dividend, so result - (result%1) drops the
#       fractional part toward zero for BOTH signs:
#         3.2  -> 3.2 - 0.2  = 3
#         3.9  -> 3.9 - 0.9  = 3
#        -3.9  -> -3.9 - (-0.9) = -3      (then later clamped to 0 by baseClamp floor)
#        -0.5  -> -0.5 - (-0.5) = 0
#         0.9999 -> 0
#   - If remainder is 0 (already integer, incl. +/-0): return result unchanged.
#   - The `result === result` guard maps NaN->0, but toFinite never returns NaN, so
#     in practice result is always finite here. Keep the guard for fidelity.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toFinite(value)  — coerce to a finite number, mapping +/-Infinity to +/-MAX_INTEGER
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toFinite(value):
#   if (!value) return value === 0 ? value : 0          # falsy: 0/-0 returned as-is, all other falsy -> 0
#   value = toNumber(value)
#   if (value === INFINITY || value === -INFINITY) {
#     sign = value < 0 ? -1 : 1
#     return sign * MAX_INTEGER                          # +Inf -> 1.7976931348623157e+308 ; -Inf -> -that
#   }
#   return value === value ? value : 0                   # NaN -> 0, else the number
#
# NOTE: within toLength, toFinite is only reached for TRUTHY values (T2 branch),
# so the `!value` guard inside toFinite is effectively dead for the toLength path
# (value is already known truthy). Reconstruct it anyway; it does not change results.
# Effect: +Infinity input -> MAX_INTEGER (~1.8e308) -> toInteger keeps it (integer) ->
# baseClamp caps to 4294967295. -Infinity -> -MAX_INTEGER -> toInteger keeps it ->
# baseClamp floors to 0.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toNumber(value)  — the string/object coercion core
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# toNumber(value):
#   if (typeof value == 'number') return value                       # numbers pass through
#   if (isSymbol(value))          return NaN                         # symbol -> NaN (out-of-envelope; see SCOPE)
#   if (isObject(value)) {                                           # object or function
#     other = (typeof value.valueOf == 'function') ? value.valueOf() : value
#     value = isObject(other) ? (other + '') : other                # if valueOf still object -> String(other)
#   }
#   if (typeof value != 'string') return value === 0 ? value : +value  # non-string -> unary +
#   # --- value is now a string ---
#   value = value.replace(/^\s+|\s+$/g, '')                          # TRIM leading/trailing whitespace
#   isBinary = /^0b[01]+$/i.test(value)
#   if (isBinary || /^0o[0-7]+$/i.test(value))
#       return parseInt(value.slice(2), isBinary ? 2 : 8)            # 0b.. base2, 0o.. base8 (strip prefix)
#   return /^[-+]0x[0-9a-f]+$/i.test(value) ? NaN : +value           # SIGNED hex -> NaN, else unary +
#
# isObject(v)     = !!v && (typeof v == 'object' || typeof v == 'function')   # null -> false
# isObjectLike(v) = !!v && typeof v == 'object'
# isSymbol(v)     = typeof v == 'symbol' || (isObjectLike(v) && Object.prototype.toString.call(v) == '[object Symbol]')
#
# STRING COERCION RULES (verified against the real package):
#   "42"        -> 42         (+ "42")
#   "  10  "    -> 10         (trim then +; whitespace-padded numerics OK)
#   "  42  "    -> 42
#   "-5"        -> -5  (toNumber) -> toInteger -5 -> baseClamp floor -> 0
#   "1e3"       -> 1000
#   "3.9"       -> 3.9 -> toInteger 3
#   ".5"        -> 0.5 -> toInteger 0
#   "1."        -> 1
#   ""          -> never reaches toNumber for toLength (falsy short-circuit -> 0)
#   "   "       -> TRUTHY string -> toNumber: trim to "" -> +"" === 0 -> toLength 0
#   "abc"       -> +"abc" = NaN -> toFinite NaN -> 0 -> toLength 0
#   "Infinity"  -> +"Infinity" = +Infinity -> toFinite -> MAX_INTEGER -> clamp 4294967295
#   "-Infinity" -> -Infinity -> toFinite -> -MAX_INTEGER -> toInteger keeps -> floor 0
#   "0x1f"      -> NOT signed -> +"0x1f" = 31         (JS hex literal coercion)
#   "0X1F"      -> NOT signed -> +"0X1F" = 31
#   "0xZZ"      -> NOT signed -> +"0xZZ" = NaN -> 0
#   "-0x1f"     -> SIGNED hex matches reIsBadHex -> NaN -> 0
#   "+0x1f"     -> SIGNED hex matches reIsBadHex -> NaN -> 0
#   "0b101"     -> binary -> parseInt("101", 2) = 5
#   "  0b101  " -> trimmed -> binary -> 5
#   "0B101"     -> binary (case-insensitive) -> 5
#   "0b102"     -> '2' not [01] -> NOT binary -> +"0b102" = NaN -> 0
#   "0o17"      -> octal -> parseInt("17", 8) = 15
#   "0o18"      -> '8' not [0-7] -> NOT octal -> +"0o18" = NaN -> 0
#
# OBJECT / NON-PRIMITIVE COERCION (verified):
#   isObject branch unboxes via valueOf, else stringifies:
#   []                 -> valueOf() returns the array (still object) -> String([])  = ""    -> +"" = 0
#                          (note: "" here is reached AFTER the toLength truthy check, since
#                           [] is truthy. trim("")="" , +"" = 0)
#   [5]                -> String([5]) = "5"   -> 5
#   [5,6]              -> String([5,6]) = "5,6" -> +"5,6" = NaN -> 0
#   {}                 -> valueOf() returns {} (object) -> String({}) = "[object Object]" -> NaN -> 0
#   {valueOf:()=>7}    -> valueOf() = 7 (number, not object) -> +7 = 7
#   new Number(42)     -> valueOf() = 42 -> 42
#   new String("99")   -> valueOf() = "99" (string) -> coerced as string -> 99
#   /ab/  (RegExp)     -> valueOf() returns regexp (object) -> String = "/ab/" -> NaN -> 0
#   new Set([1,2])     -> valueOf() returns set (object) -> String = "[object Set]" -> NaN -> 0
#   new Map()          -> String = "[object Map]" -> NaN -> 0
#   new Uint8Array([7])    -> valueOf() returns the typed array (object) -> String = "7"   -> 7
#   new Uint8Array([7,8])  -> String = "7,8" -> NaN -> 0
#   Buffer.from([9])   -> String = (byte 9 = TAB char) -> not numeric -> NaN -> 0
#                          (Buffer toString is utf8 of the bytes; non-numeric -> 0)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BIGINT BEHAVIOR (verified by running the real package)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   0n   -> 0       (BigInt zero is FALSY -> toLength T1 short-circuit -> 0; NEVER throws)
#   5n, 123n, -5n, 99999999999n, any NONZERO bigint
#        -> THROWS  TypeError: "Cannot convert a BigInt value to a number"
#   WHY: nonzero bigint is truthy -> T2 -> toInteger -> toFinite -> toNumber.
#        In toNumber: typeof 5n == 'bigint' (not 'number'), not a symbol, not an object
#        (typeof bigint == 'bigint'), not a string -> reaches `+value` (unary plus on a
#        BigInt) which THROWS TypeError per ECMAScript.
#   The blind re-emitter MUST reproduce this throw exactly for nonzero bigints
#   (recorded by the harness as {__throw}). 0n must return 0 (not throw).

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RESULT-VALUE NOTE (codec / grading)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The output is always a NON-NEGATIVE integer in [0, 4294967295], or it THROWS.
#   - It is never NaN, never negative, never a float, never -0
#     (the floor produces the literal +0).
#   - Output is always JSON-safe (a small/medium nonneg integer).

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCOPE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# IN-CONTRACT LOGIC (reproduce exactly): the entire pipeline above —
#   the falsy short-circuit, baseClamp [0, 4294967295] (cap-then-floor),
#   toInteger truncate-toward-zero, toFinite (+/-Inf -> +/-MAX_INTEGER, NaN -> 0),
#   toNumber (number passthrough, object valueOf/stringify, string trim +
#   binary/octal/signed-hex regex handling), and the nonzero-bigint TypeError throw.
#
# CARRIED DATA: none. All constants are tiny literals reproduced inline above.
#
# OUT-OF-CONTRACT: none (pure, read-only; no side effects, no seam, no global state).
#
# OUT-OF-ENVELOPE (documented, NOT emitted by genInputs because the harness codec
# cannot faithfully round-trip them — a blind re-emitter still reconstructs behavior
# from this spec):
#   - Symbol primitive (typeof 'symbol'): toNumber returns NaN -> toFinite 0 ->
#     toLength 0. (But a Symbol is TRUTHY, so it reaches toNumber; result is 0.)
#     Not round-trippable as JSON -> excluded from genInputs.
#   - Functions: truthy -> isObject true -> valueOf() returns the function (object)
#     -> String(fn) (the source text) -> NaN -> 0. Not codec-faithful -> excluded.
#   - Symbol objects, exotic host objects: same reasoning; excluded from generation.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIVERGENCES FROM THE OPERATOR HINT (documented)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hint pseudo-code: "if (!value) return 0; value = toInteger(value); if (value<0) return 0;
#                    if (value>MAX) return MAX; return value"
#   This is BEHAVIORALLY EQUIVALENT to the real source for all in-envelope inputs,
#   but the actual structure differs and these nuances must be honored:
#   1. Real code is `value ? baseClamp(toInteger(value), 0, MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH) : 0`
#      (single expression; baseClamp caps-then-floors). The hint's branch ordering
#      gives the same result but the SIR specifies the real cap-then-floor order.
#   2. "bigint throws" — TRUE ONLY FOR NONZERO bigint. `0n` is falsy -> returns 0,
#      does NOT throw. The hint over-generalized; the exact rule is in the BigInt section.
#   3. Hint omitted the rich toNumber string handling: signed-hex (reIsBadHex) -> NaN,
#      binary `0b`/octal `0o` via parseInt, unsigned `0x` hex coercion, whitespace trim,
#      and object valueOf/stringify unboxing. All specified above.
#   4. MAX_ARRAY_LENGTH = 4294967295 (= 2^32 - 1) confirmed exactly in source.
#   5. toInteger truncates TOWARD ZERO (via result - result%1), confirmed for both signs.
#   6. -0 input returns +0 (not -0): falsy short-circuit returns the literal 0.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PROVENANCE lodash.tolength@4.0.4 ; repo lodash/lodash ; MIT license ;
